      "Language": "nor",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Today, percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty (PTCA) is the most important treatment modality for coronary artery disease. However, restenosis occurs in 20-40% of the vessels in spite of the use of stents. Intravascular radiation therapy has reduced the frequency of restenosis in both animal studies and clinical trials. Recent randomized trials have shown a reduction in the order of 30% after irradiation. A number of techniques which use different sources of radiation (beta vs. gamma radiation) are under investigation. This review gives and update of intravascular radiation therapy, including a discussion of ongoing trials. There is a great need, also in Norway, for treatment modalities that reduce the frequency of restenosis after PTCA. It would seem imperative that we start a discussion of whether and when intravascular radiation therapy should be available in Norway. Implementation requires planning on a national level with regard to investment in equipment and training."},
